// The play controls and the status bar.
//
// The status bar carries the numbers that answer "is this thing working": the
// surface, the stage inside it and the scale it was fitted at, and the frame
// count. They come from the engine's own state rather than from what the editor
// asked for, so a window manager that imposed a different size shows up here
// rather than as a mystery.
//
// A button is sized from its label, never from a constant. `30` happened to hold
// "play" at the design text size and clipped it at twice that -- the bug that
// scaling the type exposed, and the reason nothing here carries a width.

// `play` toggles the simulation and reads `stop` while it is running, because a
// button that does two things has to say which one it will do next. `step`
// advances a single frame, which is the control you actually want when something
// happens too fast to see.
ed_play() {
  ed_play_hit(ed_play_btn((import sf_gw) / 2 - txt_width("play") - 12 * txt_ui(), ed_play_lbl()), 1);
  ed_play_hit(ed_play_btn((import sf_gw) / 2 + 4 * txt_ui(), "step"), 2);
} return void;

ed_play_btn(int x, string s) {
} return int ed_btn(x, ed_hdr() + 2, txt_width(s) + 8 * txt_ui(), ed_hdr(), s);

// 1 toggles running, 2 asks for one frame. Slot 16 carries both, and ed_tick
// clears a 2 after simulating -- so a step is exactly one frame however long the
// button is held down.
ed_play_hit(int ok, int k) {
  if (ok = 1) {
    lset((import ed_st), 16, ed_play_next(k));
  }
} return void;
